#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128878 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "76ff3ea8-e07c-5dba-a9af-a4f68b03fadb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5516" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/48ba234a07831383642525bf18fedcf6ea801576d80702e737d37334a04740e3.png)\n\nConsider the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}.$ First, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -7 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -10 \\\\ 5 & 9\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128879 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "4b287ac7-7569-59bc-a319-0e62996083bc" :question/math-academy-id "e-5517" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/fe3c67c0218808ba7b398d1659530d61a4c8ce3eafd196341ba6645ac7076f43.png)\n\nConsider the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ and standard basis $\\mathcal{S}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{S}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/d050ac7d04af79ad9b5e7f096e3d21c9808af4a756f43d46ca29beca00dedcc9.png)\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{S}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-7)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the standard basis $\\mathcal{S} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -7 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 13 \\\\ 3 & -9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 11 \\\\ -3 & -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128908 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/id #uuid "5e4b6303-8ec5-5ba6-ba1d-3b28ff8924b1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5897" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/76bfd9209f632ac8576051154d7e4250d8c4c53ffe843238b6d25835704ce398.png)\n\nConsider the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ of $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the matrix $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ with respect to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$, shown above. Find the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/cb0c35da5a4bd26c92343d7262efcb7ba7f197cdeea95c2d541b7441a6485804.png)\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/6759e7c098d5375894d540050e3c3dd9e3bb453b0cac7338b9b6754bb635f717.png)\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of $P_{\\small{\\mathcal{B}}\\to{\\mathcal{C}}} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-0\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C} \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 \\\\ 2 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128967 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/id #uuid "c269a1d0-a47c-5f5c-96d8-4da2ecd35a48" :question/math-academy-id "e-8563" :question/problem "Let $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}}$ and $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C},$ respectively. If $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $\\mathcal{C}$ to basis $\\mathcal{B},$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}}\\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}$\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$\n\n- $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{B} \\to \\mathcal{C}}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ relative to the bases $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{B}} = P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}} \\cdot [\\mathbf{T}]_{\\mathcal{C}} \\cdot P_{\\small \\mathcal{C} \\to \\mathcal{B}}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85d6e1b1-ebfa-5e44-bebb-0fc69b98c01f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354736} {:answer/id #uuid "e3e88f1a-5f89-55b1-b7bb-57c9fe67644d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354737} {:answer/id #uuid "803ccb35-ddae-509e-bfa2-75071a8dcff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354738} {:answer/id #uuid "f85e4112-5553-5377-99b9-e0c074e0ba04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354739} {:answer/id #uuid "f5f78a5b-cce4-5d71-8fbb-4fa8ae4bb21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5f78a5b-cce4-5d71-8fbb-4fa8ae4bb21f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354740} :answer-field/id #uuid "55874a51-b159-545c-ac87-01ec0b3336b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b23f29c7-d10d-5297-9912-f5f28cd5e901" :question/math-academy-id "q-140361" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354741 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeac26a9-d3b9-5420-aa9c-006ac43d8870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354743} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a8d21b-2d1a-56c4-852d-f5a83887acdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354744} {:answer/id #uuid "a544c2df-7789-5fb6-9ff2-f639562d61ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354745} {:answer/id #uuid "b6452795-8d54-5d8d-9ecd-ff33a2d61eaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354746} {:answer/id #uuid "f86c08ce-fe93-5cbb-9508-a24a4d9c1f5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c4a8d21b-2d1a-56c4-852d-f5a83887acdc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354744} :answer-field/id #uuid "dac652a0-bd47-55d8-9d64-b285e0ab33e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f76eee5c-930b-5c8b-bcd8-806a6dbda678" :question/math-academy-id "q-140362" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $B$ to basis $C,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89240f76-cf84-5152-8f84-ff8d14d86efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354750} {:answer/id #uuid "84c10c33-8477-5173-af44-665c312feb37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & 36 \\\\ -25 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354751} {:answer/id #uuid "ab2f9795-931f-5650-98c4-5c77aa13a6fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -25 \\\\ 20 & -36\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354752} {:answer/id #uuid "240c3118-37e8-51f6-a5ff-f32c209338e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -36 \\\\ 20 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354753} {:answer/id #uuid "55f6312c-1423-579f-9e20-0dce168ec1e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}25 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -20\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89240f76-cf84-5152-8f84-ff8d14d86efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354750} :answer-field/id #uuid "4eb6acf2-44e1-51ce-9d50-9c0215de722b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fe32d4d-9284-57a0-89e5-58bdfd98337d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140454" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-1\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -36 \\\\ 14 & -25\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354755 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b320df15-eba9-5fb5-a3da-0823cad4a3d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354757} {:answer/id #uuid "825d0da3-ad25-5cbb-85f0-24a80c26c7cc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 7 \\\\ -6 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354758} {:answer/id #uuid "d94722c7-9faf-50a2-8b4b-46ccade623ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 10 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354759} {:answer/id #uuid "45f750d5-9018-54f5-b733-6f4ef0057a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354760} {:answer/id #uuid "4ee7f521-70f7-5566-a4d9-da6f3aa6fa12" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 7 \\\\ -9 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45f750d5-9018-54f5-b733-6f4ef0057a55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354760} :answer-field/id #uuid "8602ac26-e81e-58e7-b48a-7b9b59f07e57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354756}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4d35477-b857-55bc-8644-6d12a78d9e7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-140481" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 10 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354762 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7e3c671-edb4-5f7b-bef1-4edf4309b945" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354764} {:answer/id #uuid "db4f1726-7157-59fe-96d8-86c97489d7d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -13 \\\\ 11 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354765} {:answer/id #uuid "9cbe2548-39a6-5cd8-a6dd-bc3e01a1e8f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 13 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354766} {:answer/id #uuid "13de4344-9a0a-5382-bab8-0e922ad053a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 & -11 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354767} {:answer/id #uuid "a90c4bb2-0cc7-5c76-af03-05fd53a2f555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a90c4bb2-0cc7-5c76-af03-05fd53a2f555" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354768} :answer-field/id #uuid "67239114-58f9-55e3-93ff-00aebc565907" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d85c5d1-31cb-5305-85fd-471007b641eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-140490" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -11\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & -11 & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}-{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & -13 \\\\ 6 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f2e2b12-6fb1-5d70-8061-6e4d0cc5ac5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127263} {:answer/id #uuid "d1ab0108-3bb2-51f1-b139-099a7ceee51f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127264} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf4d81b-6d53-5c58-a683-25399d0f5bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127265} {:answer/id #uuid "18c4fddb-bcb1-5a86-8164-8b51e5458ec6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127266} {:answer/id #uuid "535b2f52-557b-50b5-aab2-9e9271c4af8d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127267}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f2e2b12-6fb1-5d70-8061-6e4d0cc5ac5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127263} :answer-field/id #uuid "e631224b-9b35-5821-b674-663ee77b3cea" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127262}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d9baad5b-5623-53e5-a4c6-324293957526" :question/math-academy-id "q-38356" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-1)-2\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a26ea1f-ee44-587a-a448-6f486b3acf3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127275} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3b2d2c-0226-5569-897d-9f9b0d09e47b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127276} {:answer/id #uuid "cca81f9b-68fc-541c-b7b8-48ae8540d4a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127277} {:answer/id #uuid "40cb6dde-7047-570d-a0aa-916e6bc53b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127278} {:answer/id #uuid "5c837155-8ffd-558b-b04f-a812da0bffab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127279}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40cb6dde-7047-570d-a0aa-916e6bc53b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127278} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e159665-0abd-574a-9cad-14b642ebf7d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3ef316a2-414a-55cd-b85b-e6597918be4d" :question/math-academy-id "q-38362" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 3-5\\cdot 1}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8f30951-884b-5209-af41-72d6a477aaf6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -26 \\\\ -1 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354771} {:answer/id #uuid "5b017ce2-7ac6-5e80-86b1-78a21842ef15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 34 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354772} {:answer/id #uuid "1f8ffc8f-6d2f-556f-989c-57025b795c68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}23 & -26 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354773} {:answer/id #uuid "698dc4f6-5811-57e2-99fc-87982411ed0b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 16 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354774} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe1e884-5434-5c6b-a68e-7921d04d60df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & -16 \\\\ -1 & -4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b017ce2-7ac6-5e80-86b1-78a21842ef15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 34 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354772} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf4ebcdc-cfc0-5eba-8251-897b1c25545a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c205c90-4798-5e63-8aaa-99473a11c651" :question/math-academy-id "q-38454" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{C}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $C$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $B.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}.\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{B}.$ Solving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5-1\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -8 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 34 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354776 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d4de119-20b9-543b-8b26-82307b115ac4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354778} {:answer/id #uuid "36fe5a9e-439e-5bbc-8590-d22197e45ced" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354779} {:answer/id #uuid "b3c71e03-0342-59f9-b535-55478d8c1262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ -8 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354780} {:answer/id #uuid "ac822fbe-8fe7-5c0c-b9a3-bd9b2de450b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354781} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb8c164-e160-535a-ab56-598186ce409c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 11 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354782}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac822fbe-8fe7-5c0c-b9a3-bd9b2de450b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354781} :answer-field/id #uuid "c66cfb08-e518-561d-b80d-e0f9a0eb2b49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f01f26b9-71a9-52a6-8f28-546cd5a00ed8" :question/math-academy-id "q-38455" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 4-3\\cdot (-5)}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354783 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c05e61-2227-5d35-ad8d-ee963246804b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354785} {:answer/id #uuid "bb436f73-cc79-51eb-9925-d07bcd9f5d38" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354786} {:answer/id #uuid "d6cd9500-e94b-593e-af90-0d7b77acc008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354787} {:answer/id #uuid "96b43525-8439-5562-9696-9c10209cc15a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I