answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354790 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fb4166f-4fc1-5459-a60c-f03e9d32ec0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354792} {:answer/id #uuid "a723f0bb-be95-58e8-a7ba-0ab452d5cd73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354793} {:answer/id #uuid "88ee0ae5-23e1-5c54-8314-74ed0601a988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354794} {:answer/id #uuid "6ac73913-6466-5bf2-86ab-ea08836d1348" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354795} {:answer/id #uuid "385ce5e7-fb63-5d7f-bdb7-94d199883823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2fb4166f-4fc1-5459-a60c-f03e9d32ec0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354792} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c0f01b6-918f-5366-97a6-28687e3360e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a338424e-0f72-53da-86a1-3f28dd5a7353" :question/math-academy-id "q-97064" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}$\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}$\n- $[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/9ba3d1f0eebdfc82c40f36bdbe16896ca15516b623c695b72c331861955329bc.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354797 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "434a82c2-7964-525a-85b9-eb17fa7b5b26" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Connections Between Matrices of a Linear Transformation Relative to Different Bases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab0abe7-e399-5e89-ae9f-f99fb59e7cae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354799} {:answer/id #uuid "241b9046-28c9-5910-89c0-56f8b1280904" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354800} {:answer/id #uuid "8d06c612-c274-5929-b0f2-2e9c85acb56b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354801} {:answer/id #uuid "4096c7cd-d3c4-54b1-9021-c0e1e4bbbd58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354802} {:answer/id #uuid "b9295948-84fa-5a80-975c-3471f76227ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9295948-84fa-5a80-975c-3471f76227ca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354803} :answer-field/id #uuid "76f9ed26-15a1-5bf7-8699-6956084fca89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354798}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66bac7f0-bebb-5539-9523-e8c242e09305" :question/math-academy-id "q-97066" :question/problem "Let $[T]_{B}$ and $[T]_{C}$ be the matrices of a linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C,$ respectively. If ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from basis $C$ to basis $B,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $[T]_{B}=[T]_{C}^{-1}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}$\n- $[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}$\n- $[T]_{C}=[T]_{B}^{-1}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nSolving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "678c0e0f-0e8c-5c18-a2ab-a16ba5594810" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 6 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354806} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9b95a2-e521-5fc9-8d0e-86015b90ba9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 18 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354807} {:answer/id #uuid "3c226226-225b-5dcc-87e2-184bb4ab3550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354808} {:answer/id #uuid "f631ca61-fb74-55b1-829e-1d2b43dda7d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354809} {:answer/id #uuid "ca51e8bf-88ba-5394-904f-988e8ddefe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ 18 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed9b95a2-e521-5fc9-8d0e-86015b90ba9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 18 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354807} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ec928bf-c7ed-5ac5-ae66-0369aaeeb40d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5b5c269-5ad9-54fa-b153-20584bb6cfef" :question/math-academy-id "q-97111" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 0}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -1 \\\\ 18 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354811 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da1b8239-8d13-503d-b523-fb390ee7963d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -16 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354813} {:answer/id #uuid "a22fac5f-c21a-5af7-a68b-eda1d1b29b11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354814} {:answer/id #uuid "241eda8e-332a-52be-9d61-3214d30067a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354815} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6f0664-3583-5dfb-b141-0b5ee013bbe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354816} {:answer/id #uuid "f3921790-347c-5828-bd1e-5177a0c77fa2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}14 & -20 \\\\ -12 & 17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "241eda8e-332a-52be-9d61-3214d30067a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354815} :answer-field/id #uuid "e102fefd-564f-5a23-acf2-838a3f60a4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354812}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8232ae8-3dbc-5fa7-a890-a6276b10aff3" :question/math-academy-id "q-97114" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{C}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $C$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{C\\to B}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $B.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/2e82c232614bec8a98f079ea56d7913ee13e7b457e33e418198278d2e369fc5c.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}.\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{C\\to B}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{B}.$ Solving this equation for $[T]_{B},$ we obtain the alternative formulation\n\n$$\n[T]_{B}={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 3-2\\cdot 4}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{B} & ={P}_{C\\to B}\\cdot [T]_{C}\\cdot {P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06ecc06b-e22f-5cfd-b37c-8640e43370c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 8 \\\\ -9 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354820} {:answer/id #uuid "bce29b34-ee54-5468-814f-4483c72d882f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 10 \\\\ -5 & 13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354821} {:answer/id #uuid "757f912c-6973-58b0-9dd2-65580058f567" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 16 \\\\ -11 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354822} {:answer/id #uuid "94651103-b46c-5b71-a3bc-c0dd024faffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354823} {:answer/id #uuid "5ab341c1-fcc9-51ea-823c-b9945fc813e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94651103-b46c-5b71-a3bc-c0dd024faffa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354823} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ea4f3e-0cc1-5453-91f6-aba73a8c8545" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34bf2c09-8cfb-5207-bf6e-8fe44cc2d28e" :question/math-academy-id "q-97119" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot (-3)-(-1)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354825 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d5419d7-3650-5809-8e32-6c6090b68653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354827} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1da4c9-3f20-5a3c-aaaa-b605a81eee4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354828} {:answer/id #uuid "5820bd7f-e521-5c00-8402-6242a64b7fe9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 \\\\ 23 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354829} {:answer/id #uuid "f5568fe6-e6ea-52e8-b0ae-4c923979060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -23 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354830} {:answer/id #uuid "ce8ef583-ae84-5fcd-9843-0ff181bfea23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 23 & -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354831}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5568fe6-e6ea-52e8-b0ae-4c923979060d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -23 & 10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354830} :answer-field/id #uuid "d13d31e1-69d8-539b-8c8b-8ab9e5ea7ef1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354826}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc7f6482-f87f-5b59-92e8-8de06bb91dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-97122" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-2)\\cdot 2-(-1)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ -23 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354832 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6497469-aa12-5d91-9dad-4f9c968a39a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -12 \\\\ -10 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354834} {:answer/id #uuid "ca53dc20-3b7b-5acc-8cb6-126730beceb5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354835} {:answer/id #uuid "1c2389bb-dc14-5d9f-a052-2323d50d1119" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354836} {:answer/id #uuid "a766d07e-2dd1-5ef5-b7d6-53c319e760fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354837} {:answer/id #uuid "b3b07ebc-bf23-5dad-82ab-bc750b5fb656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3b07ebc-bf23-5dad-82ab-bc750b5fb656" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354838} :answer-field/id #uuid "f19af110-7ee7-5da9-8def-b08086e454be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c615bf54-fc91-5d1c-a18a-12610944ff51" :question/math-academy-id "q-97278" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354839 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf884265-be6c-5778-970a-24ff1fc3578b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354841} {:answer/id #uuid "482da4b9-18e3-53f9-acc9-3d4625edc8cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354842} {:answer/id #uuid "de27e708-6a87-55b5-9708-1a7ae9237642" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-11 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354843} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ff5875-227f-5201-b483-3adb78931519" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}20 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354844} {:answer/id #uuid "00381990-0a39-5cfb-8126-d283081d8bbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -13\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354845}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf884265-be6c-5778-970a-24ff1fc3578b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354841} :answer-field/id #uuid "127f4f10-9e66-52da-8e27-2f21d7414e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "150207d8-196e-5c9f-bf3a-465dd9f4f1f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-97280" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 8 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 8 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -11 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-1)\\cdot (-11)-4\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ -4 & -11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 11\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5c02428-046e-57e3-b131-913676e9c806" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354848} {:answer/id #uuid "7971f195-b0a6-56d5-a407-d5142d484820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354849} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d5af78-73ce-573c-ab16-8882f11b32fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -6 \\\\ 6 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354850} {:answer/id #uuid "bb7b5d03-e56a-52a6-9a33-d646a4283ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354851} {:answer/id #uuid "742022a8-bd35-5eb7-8c5d-7c5631262619" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354852}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb7b5d03-e56a-52a6-9a33-d646a4283ea4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354851} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f4cffae-3058-57f0-9388-bd5b08560299" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "90dac6d1-6fdb-5236-89cf-8823a20cc32a" :question/math-academy-id "q-97281" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -3 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-2)-(-3)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354853 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7f78628-af29-5273-ab8e-31e0506df01e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 4 \\\\ -8 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354855} {:answer/id #uuid "a223c353-5da9-572b-b193-459ce6657910" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 7 \\\\ -7 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354856} {:answer/id #uuid "55a8f562-43ab-51cd-8e8a-c7dff127c750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354857} {:answer/id #uuid "f135668f-ff7c-540b-9a08-9ee64d3cbb86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354858} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd961d8-8219-57af-8ec5-3905df9ccc8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -7 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354859}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55a8f562-43ab-51cd-8e8a-c7dff127c750" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354857} :answer-field/id #uuid "761b540a-adb6-56c7-a801-13e3d98bfedd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186354854}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c7c77f8-d354-5712-9d15-cc66aa1dd115" :question/math-academy-id "q-97283" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14067267/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -14 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 10 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -7 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 3-(-7)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 7 \\\\ 21 & -15\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
